[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"JUnit: Writing Unit Tests, Assertions, and Test Fixtures"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Unit testing is a crucial part of software development. It involves testing individual units or components of your code in isolation to ensure they function as expected. JUnit is a popular Java testing framework that provides a structured way to write and run unit tests."},{"order":3,"type":"heading","text":"Setting Up JUnit"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"Before you can start writing JUnit tests, you need to add the JUnit library to your project. If you are using Maven, add the following dependency to your `pom.xml` file:"},{"order":5,"type":"code","text":"<dependency>
    <groupId>org.junit.jupiter</groupId>
    <artifactId>junit-jupiter-api</artifactId>
    <version>5.10.0</version>
    <scope>test</scope>
</dependency>"},{"order":6,"type":"paragraph","text":"If you are using Gradle, add the following dependency to your `build.gradle` file:"},{"order":7,"type":"code","text":"dependencies {
    testImplementation 'org.junit.jupiter:junit-jupiter-api:5.10.0'
    testRuntimeOnly 'org.junit.jupiter:junit-jupiter-engine:5.10.0'
}"},{"order":8,"type":"paragraph","text":"Make sure to refresh your project dependencies after adding JUnit to your project."},{"order":9,"type":"heading","text":"Writing Your First JUnit Test"},{"order":10,"type":"paragraph","text":"Let's start with a simple example. Suppose you have a class called `Calculator` with a method `add` that adds two numbers:"},{"order":11,"type":"code","text":"public class Calculator {
    public int add(int a, int b) {
        return a + b;
    }
}"},{"order":12,"type":"paragraph","text":"To test this class, create a new class (e.g., `CalculatorTest`) in your test directory. Annotate the test methods with `@Test`."},{"order":13,"type":"code","text":"import org.junit.jupiter.api.Test;
import static org.junit.jupiter.api.Assertions.*;

public class CalculatorTest {

    @Test
    public void testAdd() {
        Calculator calculator = new Calculator();
        int result = calculator.add(2, 3);
        assertEquals(5, result);
    }
}"},{"order":14,"type":"paragraph","text":"In this example:"},{"order":15,"type":"bullet_point","text":"`@Test` annotation marks the `testAdd` method as a test case."},{"order":16,"type":"bullet_point","text":"`assertEquals(5, result)` is an assertion that checks if the expected value (5) is equal to the actual result."},{"order":17,"type":"heading","text":"Assertions in JUnit"},{"order":18,"type":"paragraph","text":"JUnit provides a variety of assertion methods to verify different conditions. Here are some commonly used assertions:"},{"order":19,"type":"bullet_point","text":"`assertEquals(expected, actual)`: Checks if two values are equal."},{"order":20,"type":"bullet_point","text":"`assertNotEquals(unexpected, actual)`: Checks if two values are not equal."},{"order":21,"type":"bullet_point","text":"`assertTrue(condition)`: Checks if a condition is true."},{"order":22,"type":"bullet_point","text":"`assertFalse(condition)`: Checks if a condition is false."},{"order":23,"type":"bullet_point","text":"`assertNull(object)`: Checks if an object is null."},{"order":24,"type":"bullet_point","text":"`assertNotNull(object)`: Checks if an object is not null."},{"order":25,"type":"bullet_point","text":"`assertSame(expected, actual)`: Checks if two objects refer to the same instance."},{"order":26,"type":"bullet_point","text":"`assertNotSame(unexpected, actual)`: Checks if two objects do not refer to the same instance."},{"order":27,"type":"bullet_point","text":"`assertThrows(expectedType, executable)`: Checks if the execution of the supplied executable throws an exception of the expected type."},{"order":28,"type":"paragraph","text":"Example using `assertTrue`:"},{"order":29,"type":"code","text":"@Test
public void testIsPositive() {
    assertTrue(5 > 0);
}"},{"order":30,"type":"paragraph","text":"Example using `assertNull`:"},{"order":31,"type":"code","text":"@Test
public void testObjectIsNull() {
    Object obj = null;
    assertNull(obj);
}"},{"order":32,"type":"heading","text":"Test Fixtures"},{"order":33,"type":"paragraph","text":"Test fixtures are a fixed state of the software under test used as a baseline for running tests. JUnit provides annotations to set up and tear down test fixtures:"},{"order":34,"type":"bullet_point","text":"`@BeforeEach`: Executes before each test method."},{"order":35,"type":"bullet_point","text":"`@AfterEach`: Executes after each test method."},{"order":36,"type":"bullet_point","text":"`@BeforeAll`: Executes once before all test methods in the class.  Must be static."},{"order":37,"type":"bullet_point","text":"`@AfterAll`: Executes once after all test methods in the class. Must be static."},{"order":38,"type":"paragraph","text":"Example:"},{"order":39,"type":"code","text":"import org.junit.jupiter.api.*;

public class CalculatorTest {

    private Calculator calculator;

    @BeforeEach
    public void setUp() {
        calculator = new Calculator();
        System.out.println("Setting up calculator...");
    }

    @AfterEach
    public void tearDown() {
        System.out.println("Tearing down calculator...");
    }

    @Test
    public void testAdd() {
        int result = calculator.add(2, 3);
        assertEquals(5, result);
    }

    @Test
    public void testSubtract() {
        int result = calculator.add(5, 2);
        assertEquals(7, result);
    }

    @BeforeAll
    static void beforeAll() {
        System.out.println("Starting tests...");
    }

    @AfterAll
    static void afterAll() {
        System.out.println("Tests finished...");
    }
}"},{"order":40,"type":"paragraph","text":"In this example, `@BeforeEach` is used to create a new `Calculator` instance before each test, and `@AfterEach` is used to perform cleanup after each test. `@BeforeAll` and `@AfterAll` are used to perform setup and teardown operations that apply to all tests in the class."},{"order":41,"type":"heading","text":"Testing for Exceptions"},{"order":42,"type":"paragraph","text":"Sometimes you need to verify that your code throws an exception under certain conditions. JUnit provides the `assertThrows` method for this purpose."},{"order":43,"type":"code","text":"import org.junit.jupiter.api.Test;

import static org.junit.jupiter.api.Assertions.assertThrows;

public class ExceptionTest {

    @Test
    public void testException() {
        assertThrows(IllegalArgumentException.class, () -> {
            throw new IllegalArgumentException("Invalid argument");
        });
    }
}"},{"order":44,"type":"paragraph","text":"This test verifies that the code inside the lambda expression throws an `IllegalArgumentException`."},{"order":45,"type":"heading","text":"Conclusion"},{"order":46,"type":"paragraph","text":"JUnit is a powerful tool for writing unit tests in Java. By using assertions and test fixtures, you can create comprehensive tests that ensure the correctness of your code. Remember to write tests that cover different scenarios, including edge cases and exceptions, to improve the reliability of your software."}]
